---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { siteData } from "../lib/data";
import { clipCount, failedModels, generatedModels, imageCount } from "../lib/models";
import { path } from "../lib/paths";

const models = generatedModels(siteData, "image");
const failed = failedModels(siteData, "image");
const imageTotal = models.reduce((n, m) => n + imageCount(m), 0);
const soundModels = generatedModels(siteData, "audio");
const soundFailed = failedModels(siteData, "audio");
const clipTotal = soundModels.reduce((n, m) => n + clipCount(m), 0);
const generatedAt = siteData.generated_at.slice(0, 10);
---

<Base
  title="About — Crungus Amongus"
  description="What the crungus is, how this archive was made, and what the consistency score does and doesn't mean."
>
  <article>
    <h1>About</h1>

    <p>
      Crungus Amongus combines a comparative study of model outputs with an interactive audiovisual archive.
      It investigates how consistently different models respond to prompts containing “crungus”, and what similarities appear across their outputs.
      Shared prompts make those differences available for inspection.
    </p>
    <p>
      Browse the <a href={path()}>images by model</a>, explore visual similarities in the
      <a href={path("/atlas/")}>atlas</a>, or examine variation with the <a href={path("/eigen/")}>eigencrungi sliders</a>.
      The <a href={path("/mosaic/")}>display</a> fills a screen with images drawn at random, and <a href={path("/mosaic/?radio")}>crungus radio</a> plays independently shuffled sounds over it.
    </p>
    <h2>The word</h2>
    <p>
      In June 2022, comedian Guy Kelly shared a grid of nine DALL·E mini images generated from the word “crungus”.
      Several resembled a hairy, horned creature. The images became known as a
      <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crungus">digital cryptid</a>.
      This project uses that recurring resemblance as a starting point for comparing models.
    </p>
    <h2>The archive</h2>
    <p>
      Models are drawn from Replicate's <a href="https://replicate.com/collections/text-to-image">text-to-image collection</a>
      and a curated selection of older models. This is a sample of available endpoints, including related models and fine-tunes.
      It is not an exhaustive survey or a set of independent model families.
    </p>
    <p>
      The image experiment requests 100 outputs per model for each of two prompts: “crungus” and “a picture of a crungus”.
      We supply no stylistic instructions or fixed seeds. Endpoint defaults and required inputs vary between models.
      Where an endpoint exposes safety controls, we use the most permissive settings available.
      As of {generatedAt}, the archive holds {imageTotal.toLocaleString("en-AU")} images from {models.length} models.
      {failed.length > 0 && ` ${failed.length} additional models were attempted without producing an image.`}
    </p>
    <p>
      Timeline years identify model releases, using curated dates where available and Replicate version dates as a fallback.
      The images were generated for this project; they are not historical samples collected in those release years.
      The homepage filmstrip selects the image with the highest average CLIP similarity to the other images in each release-year group.
    </p>
    <h2>The sound</h2>
    <p>
      The audio experiment requests ten outputs for each of “crungus” and “the sound of a crungus”.
      Models come from Replicate's <a href="https://replicate.com/collections/ai-music-generation">music collection</a> and a curated selection including Bark.
      We request twenty-second clips where duration is configurable; other endpoints set their own length.
      Required lyrics use “crungus”, repeated where necessary to meet input constraints.
      Clips are encoded and loudness-normalised for playback.
      {clipTotal > 0 ? ` The archive holds ${clipTotal} clips from ${soundModels.length} models.` : " The sound archive is still being recorded."}
      {soundFailed.length > 0 && ` ${soundFailed.length} additional models were attempted without producing a clip.`}
    </p>
    <h2 id="consistency">Consistency</h2>
    <p>
      Consistency is the mean pairwise cosine similarity between a model's output embeddings for one prompt.
      Images use CLIP embeddings; audio uses <a href="https://github.com/LAION-AI/CLAP">CLAP</a> embeddings.
      A high score can reflect recurring subjects, composition or style. It does not establish that a model represents a particular creature.
      Even a model that always produces a beige void could score highly.
    </p>
    <p>
      Archive cards show the higher of a model's two prompt scores; model pages show each prompt separately.
      The scores describe similarity within each model's outputs, not agreement between models or output quality.
      Image and audio scores use different embedding models and should be compared within their respective collections.
    </p>
    <h2>Refusals</h2>
    <p>
      Early runs used each endpoint's default safety settings. The refusal figures on model pages record those runs,
      measured before the configuration changed on 9 September 2026.
      Subsequent runs use the most permissive safety settings each endpoint exposes; provider restrictions may still apply.
      The historical refusal rates do not describe the images currently displayed.
    </p>
    <h2>The atlas</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href={path("/atlas/")}>atlas</a> projects CLIP image embeddings into two dimensions using UMAP.
      Nearby images can share subjects, colours or composition.
      Apparent clusters and the gaps between them depend on the projection: <a href="https://umap-learn.readthedocs.io/en/latest/clustering.html">UMAP can introduce separations</a>.
      The map supports visual exploration; it does not establish distinct categories of creature.
    </p>
    <h2>The eigencrungi</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href={path("/eigen/")}>pixel sliders</a> combine a mean image with principal components fitted to the archive.
      Images are centre-cropped and resized to 128 × 128 pixels, without aligning subjects.
      The components capture variation in colour and framing as well as depicted subjects. Combining the retained components gives an approximation of an archive image.
    </p>
    <p>
      The CLIP features mode applies principal component analysis to ViT-bigG-14 image embeddings, each containing 1,280 values.
      Kandinsky 2.2 renders selected positions along individual components. These previews are the decoder's interpretations;
      compare them with the nearby archive images shown alongside. Kandinsky is also one of the models represented in the collection.
    </p>
    <p>
      Stability measures how closely a component reproduces across disjoint halves of the archive, averaged over eight splits.
      We use a cosine similarity threshold of 0.7 when naming components and rendering their filmstrips.
      This is a working threshold, not a boundary between meaningful features and noise. Current scores appear beside the sliders.
    </p>
    <p>
      The separate “crungus-ness” slider uses image similarity to CLIP's text embedding of “crungus”.
      It measures an association in CLIP, distinct from the within-model consistency score.
      Its pixel visualisation is fitted from the archive by least squares, so that visualisation depends on the sampled images.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href={path("/technical-notes/")}>Technical notes</a> document earlier sample-size experiments, component rotation,
      decoder configuration and generation costs. Those historical observations are kept separate from the current component scores.
    </p>

    <h2>Prior sightings</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crungus">Crungus</a> on
        Wikipedia
      </li>
      <li>
        Bruce Sterling's{" "}
        <a href="https://www.flickr.com/photos/brucesterling/albums/72177720324124391/">
        Flickr archive</a> of the original Craiyon screenshots
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://artificialanalysis.ai/image/explore">Artificial
        Analysis</a>, for comparisons of image models
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The data</h2>
    <p>
      The archive is also published as static JSON, readable from any origin.{" "}
      <a href={path("/api/models.json")}><code>/api/models.json</code></a> lists
      the models, <a href={path("/api/images.json")}><code>/api/images.json</code></a>{" "}
      and <a href={path("/api/audio.json")}><code>/api/audio.json</code></a> list
      every image and clip, and <code>/api/models/&lt;slug&gt;.json</code> holds
      one model's outputs. Each entry is a link to the file with the model and
      prompt that produced it. To sample an output, fetch a list and select an entry.
    </p>

    <h2>Colophon</h2>
    <p class="mono">
      images and clips generated via the Replicate API · scored with
      open-clip (ViT-B/32) and LAION CLAP · projected with UMAP · decomposed
      with numpy · encoded as
      AVIF and Opus
      (with an AAC fallback) · served from Tigris object storage · built with
      Astro · made by{" "}
      <a href="https://benswift.me">Ben Swift</a> at the ANU School of
      Cybernetics
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  article {
    max-width: 46rem;
    padding-block: 2rem 3rem;
  }

  h1 {
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 2.8rem);
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
  }

  h2 {
    font-size: 1.3rem;
    margin: 2rem 0 0.5rem;
  }

  ul {
    padding-inline-start: 1.2rem;
  }

  li {
    margin-block-end: 0.3rem;
  }
</style>
